 ## Background
-Origin: The foundational principles of ray casting and front-to-back compositing were established in the early 1980s by Williams et al. and Levoy, forming the basis of Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) [S1, unofficial]. The specific adaptive segment length strategy cited in [S1, unofficial] references Quatrin Campagnolo et al. [9] for optimizing speed by varying segment length based on color contribution.
+The concept of ray traversal and integration is fundamental to image-order volume rendering, originating from the work of Williams et al. (1989) on ray casting [G]. The specific reduction schemes (MIP, average, accumulation) are standard techniques in medical imaging and scientific visualization, widely adopted in early GPU-based volume rendering pipelines [G]. The distinction between traversal (finding samples) and integration (compositing samples) became explicit as hardware accelerated the former, allowing the latter to be optimized via reduction operations [G].
 
